{"agent_id":"pp3g-8ppZ-delta-re-review-of-the-finalized-workflow-topology","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-08T00:03:36.233295319Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"e94bd364-b6f9-4505-ae53-13f1912f1966","comment_uuid":"5c61121a-1494-4cb7-a70a-1f112453e115","author":"pp3g-8ppZ-delta-re-review-of-the-finalized-workflow-topology","content":"## RESULT (corrected): DELTA RE-REVIEW — FINALIZED workflow-topology design\n\nVerdict: **DISAGREE-with-specific-findings** on one delta; otherwise AGREE.\n\n### Finding 1 (disagreement): AUDITOR should not be the divergence-verifier label\n\nThe finalized design assigns the **AUDITOR** role to trigger-invoked, read-only divergence verification. I disagree with that role-label choice.\n\n- docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md defines the Auditor as an independent evaluator of **completed work** from a project/architectural/process perspective. Divergence verification is a live, operational watchdog activity, not a post-hoc project audit.\n- Conflating the two weakens the Auditor's independence and creates role confusion: a divergence verifier needs operational access to the position store and artifact evidence; an Auditor is supposed to stand outside implementation and review.\n- docs/standards/Concept - Levels of Abstraction.md supports this separation: real-time state monitoring belongs at the Architecture/Implementation layer, while the Auditor is a Methodology-layer oversight role.\n\nSuggested resolution: introduce a distinct agent (e.g., **Verifier** or **Watcher**) for trigger-invoked divergence checks, and keep **Auditor** for periodic methodological/architectural audits. If the project intentionally redefines Auditor, update docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md, the permission matrix, and the agent definitions explicitly; do not let the change happen by implication.\n\n### Finding 2 (clarification, not a hard disagreement): the reviewer gate needs a sharper label\n\nCalling the reviewer gate a **“pre-consumption testability audit (L1)”** is narrower than the design's own principles. Reasoning-certainty and cheapest-test-first require evidence that cheapest discriminating tests were actually run and that certainty labels are present, not merely that the artifact is “testable.” If L1 is the load-bearing pre-consumption gate, its acceptance criteria must include both testability and calibration evidence.\n\nSuggested resolution: rename to **“pre-consumption readiness audit”** and enumerate pass criteria (why/what/certainty stated + cheapest-test evidence present).\n\n### Elements I agree with\n\n- **(b) Position-emitting agents + durable store + task-adaptive cadence** — sound, provided the store is append-only and the expected-interval baseline is explicit per task class.\n- **(c) Orchestrator as single integration point** — consistent with docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md; preserves the thin-orchestrator principle if it only handles decisions/synthesis, not all data flow.\n- **(d) Model-agreement with external correctness anchor + non-delivery as first-class outcome** — sound and aligned with the draft schema on #241; the external-anchor reminder is especially important.\n\n### Overall\n\nThe topology is coherent and the delta changes fix the first-wave issues visible in the synthesis. The only substantive disagreement is the **Auditor-as-divergence-verifier** label, which I believe violates existing role and abstraction boundaries. Fix that naming/scope issue and the design holds.","created_at":"2026-08-08T00:03:36.227290485Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:cDJqOZ0cXmoMdD3E1LCg3CvhQpJbSdztQdcCUQL6xQo","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgEWTP3gZHiv/cKDr4NnW/y+Hn88RC0UgGf1E3shFSjacAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAtxlC3hpc/jhz2ODVd0AiL69WpMpFQ/OY2/PSnKvuGYH0SiIx0NJVL+8BD2CQ6AIYyikmTdis9ZgsitX8D438Ag=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:cDJqOZ0cXmoMdD3E1LCg3CvhQpJbSdztQdcCUQL6xQo","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgEWTP3gZHiv/cKDr4NnW/y+Hn8